window.NET_CARDS = [{"n":0.1,"ti":"Bytes, Encoding & Byte Order","f":"p1-bytes-and-encoding.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What is len(\"naïve\".encode(\"utf-8\")) ?","o":["5","6","7","10"],"a":1,"w":"Five code points, but ï (U+00EF) is outside ASCII, so UTF-8 encodes it in 2 bytes: 4 × 1 + 2 = 6."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Bytes, Encoding & Byte Order","f":"p1-bytes-and-encoding.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Bytes 2–3 of a TCP header (the destination port) are 01 bb . Which port is it?","o":["187","47873","80","443"],"a":3,"w":"Header fields are big-endian: 0x01BB = 1 × 256 + 187 = 443. Reading them little-endian would give 0xBB01 = 47873, the classic forgotten- ntohs bug."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Bytes, Encoding & Byte Order","f":"p1-bytes-and-encoding.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A service displays a customer's name as \"JosÃ©\". What most likely happened?","o":["UTF-8 bytes were decoded with a single-byte encoding such as Latin-1","The network corrupted the packet","The bytes were sent little-endian","The name was truncated by Content-Length"],"a":0,"w":"é is c3 a9 in UTF-8. Latin-1 maps c3 to \"Ã\" and a9 to \"©\". Single-byte decoders never fail, so the bug shows up as garbage text instead of an error."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Bytes, Encoding & Byte Order","f":"p1-bytes-and-encoding.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Ignoring protocol overhead, what is the best-case transfer rate of a 100 Mbit/s link?","o":["100 MB/s","800 MB/s","12.5 MB/s","1.25 MB/s"],"a":2,"w":"Rates are quoted in bits. 100 Mbit/s ÷ 8 bits per byte = 12.5 MB/s, before headers, retransmissions and the rest of the stack take their share."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Bytes, Encoding & Byte Order","f":"p1-bytes-and-encoding.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does struct.pack(\"!I\", 0xC0A8010A) return?","o":["b'\\x0a\\x01\\xa8\\xc0'","b'\\xc0\\xa8\\x01\\x0a'","The string \"192.168.1.10\"","It depends on the CPU"],"a":1,"w":"! means network order, big-endian, on every CPU: most significant byte first. Those four bytes are how the IPv4 address 192.168.1.10 appears in a packet header ( P2 )."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"IP Addresses & Binary Arithmetic","f":"p2-ip-arithmetic.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What 32-bit integer is the address 0.0.1.0 ?","o":["1","16","256","65,536"],"a":2,"w":"The third octet is worth 2⁸ = 256 per unit, so 0.0.1.0 = 1 × 256 = 256. (0.1.0.0 would be 65,536.)"},{"n":0.2,"ti":"IP Addresses & Binary Arithmetic","f":"p2-ip-arithmetic.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What is the subnet mask for a /20 prefix?","o":["255.255.240.0","255.255.255.240","255.255.20.0","255.240.0.0"],"a":0,"w":"20 ones: 8 + 8 fill the first two octets with 255, and the remaining 4 ones make the third octet 11110000 = 240."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"IP Addresses & Binary Arithmetic","f":"p2-ip-arithmetic.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which network does 172.20.47.200/20 belong to?","o":["172.20.47.0","172.20.32.0","172.20.0.0","172.20.40.0"],"a":1,"w":"The mask splits the third octet: 47 AND 240 = 00101111 AND 11110000 = 00100000 = 32. Equivalently, blocks of 16, and 47 is in the block starting at 32."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"IP Addresses & Binary Arithmetic","f":"p2-ip-arithmetic.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which of these addresses is not in a private (RFC 1918) range?","o":["10.255.0.1","172.31.200.7","192.168.100.100","172.32.0.1"],"a":3,"w":"172.16.0.0/12 runs from 172.16.0.0 to 172.31.255.255. 172.32.0.1 is one past the end, so it's a public address."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"IP Addresses & Binary Arithmetic","f":"p2-ip-arithmetic.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Host 10.0.1.20/24 sends a packet to 10.0.2.9. What does it do?","o":["ARPs for 10.0.2.9 and sends directly","Broadcasts the packet on the LAN","Sends it to its default gateway, because 10.0.2.9 is on another network","Drops it: 10.0.2.9 is unreachable"],"a":2,"w":"10.0.2.9 AND 255.255.255.0 = 10.0.2.0, which differs from the host's own network 10.0.1.0, so the packet goes to the gateway (whose MAC the host finds with ARP)."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Clients, Servers & Ports","f":"p3-clients-servers-ports.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which values identify one TCP connection?","o":["The server's IP address and port","Protocol, source IP, source port, destination IP, destination port","The client's MAC address and the server's port","The process ID of the server"],"a":1,"w":"The 5-tuple. Change any one value and it's a different connection, which is how many clients share one server port."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Clients, Servers & Ports","f":"p3-clients-servers-ports.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"You open two browser tabs to the same HTTPS site. How does the server tell the two connections apart?","o":["It gives each tab a different server port","By a cookie in the first packet","The laptop's OS picked a different source port for each, so the 5-tuples differ","It can't; both tabs share one connection"],"a":2,"w":"Everything else is identical, so the source port is what makes each 5-tuple unique. (Browsers may also reuse one connection for both tabs, but if they open two, this is how they're told apart.)"},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Clients, Servers & Ports","f":"p3-clients-servers-ports.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What happens when a client sends a TCP SYN to a port where nothing is listening?","o":["The host replies with a reset (RST) and the client reports \"connection refused\"","The packet waits until a program listens","The next free port is used instead","The client always times out"],"a":0,"w":"A closed port is answered immediately with RST. A timeout usually means a firewall dropped the SYN silently, a useful difference when debugging ( P4 )."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Clients, Servers & Ports","f":"p3-clients-servers-ports.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"With Linux's default ephemeral range, roughly how many simultaneous connections can one client IP open to 10.0.0.5:443 ?","o":["1,024","65,535","Unlimited","About 28,000"],"a":3,"w":"Only the source port can vary, and the default range 32768–60999 has 28,232 ports. To go further, add client IPs, server addresses or ports, or widen the range."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Clients, Servers & Ports","f":"p3-clients-servers-ports.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"In ss -tan on a server, a line reads LISTEN 0 511 0.0.0.0:443 0.0.0.0:* . What is it?","o":["A client connected from 0.0.0.0","A listening socket accepting connections on port 443 on every interface","A connection waiting to close","A UDP socket"],"a":1,"w":"0.0.0.0 as the local address means \"all interfaces\", and * as the peer means there is no peer: it's a listener. 511 is the size of its queue of completed connections waiting for accept() ."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"ping, curl, dig & Friends","f":"p4-network-cli-tools.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"curl prints Failed to connect to api.example.com port 443: Connection refused . What do you know?","o":["DNS failed","The host is reachable, but nothing is listening on port 443 there","A firewall silently dropped the packets","The certificate is invalid"],"a":1,"w":"\"Refused\" means a reset came back, so the packets reached a host that has no listening socket on that port. A silent drop would produce a timeout instead."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"ping, curl, dig & Friends","f":"p4-network-cli-tools.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"ping to a host works, but curl to port 443 hangs and then reports a timeout. What's the most likely cause?","o":["The web server isn't running","DNS returned the wrong address","A firewall or security group drops TCP 443 while allowing ICMP","The TLS certificate expired"],"a":2,"w":"The host is reachable (ping), and nothing answers the SYN at all, not even a reset. Something between is dropping packets to that port."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"ping, curl, dig & Friends","f":"p4-network-cli-tools.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"dig shows status: NXDOMAIN . What does it mean?","o":["The name does not exist","The DNS server is down","The name exists but has no A record","The answer was too large for UDP"],"a":0,"w":"NXDOMAIN is an authoritative \"no such name\". A name that exists without that record type gives NOERROR with an empty answer, and a resolver that can't get any answer returns SERVFAIL."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"ping, curl, dig & Friends","f":"p4-network-cli-tools.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"In curl -v output, what do lines starting with > show?","o":["Response headers","TLS handshake messages","Errors","The request line and headers curl sent"],"a":3,"w":"> is outgoing (the request), < is incoming (the response), and * is curl's own commentary on DNS, TCP and TLS."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"ping, curl, dig & Friends","f":"p4-network-cli-tools.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A reply shows ttl=54 from a Linux server. Roughly how many routers did it cross?","o":["54","About 10","About 200","It's the round-trip time in ms"],"a":1,"w":"TTL is a hop counter, not a time. Linux starts at 64 and each router subtracts 1, so 64 − 54 = 10 hops (Windows starts at 128, many routers at 255)."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Sockets in Python","f":"p5-python-sockets.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does srv.accept() return?","o":["The first bytes the client sent","True once a client is connected","A new socket for that one connection, and the client's (IP, port)","The listening socket, now connected"],"a":2,"w":"The listening socket keeps listening. Each accepted connection gets its own socket, which is the one you recv and send on."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Sockets in Python","f":"p5-python-sockets.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A blocking TCP socket's recv(4096) returns b\"\" . What happened?","o":["The peer closed its side of the connection","No data has arrived yet; call again","The timeout expired","The buffer was too small"],"a":0,"w":"A blocking recv waits for data, so an empty result can only mean end of stream: the other side sent FIN. A timeout raises TimeoutError instead."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Sockets in Python","f":"p5-python-sockets.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why use sendall(data) instead of send(data) ?","o":["sendall is faster","send doesn't work on TCP","sendall waits for the peer to acknowledge the data","send may queue only part of the data and return how many bytes it took; sendall loops until all of it is queued"],"a":3,"w":"When the kernel send buffer is nearly full, send takes what fits. Neither call waits for the peer's acknowledgement; both return once the bytes are in the local kernel's buffer."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Sockets in Python","f":"p5-python-sockets.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A UDP client calls sendto(b\"ping\", (\"10.0.0.5\", 9001)) but nothing listens on that port. What does the client see?","o":["ConnectionRefusedError from sendto","sendto succeeds; the following recvfrom times out (if a timeout is set) or blocks forever","An empty datagram back","sendto blocks until a listener appears"],"a":1,"w":"UDP has no handshake, so sending can't discover that nobody is there. That's why UDP clients always need a timeout, and usually retries."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Sockets in Python","f":"p5-python-sockets.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Your server restarts and fails with OSError: [Errno 98] Address already in use , though the old process has exited. What helps?","o":["Using UDP instead","Calling listen() before bind()","Setting SO_REUSEADDR on the listening socket before bind()","A bigger listen backlog"],"a":2,"w":"Connections from the previous run are still in TIME_WAIT on that port. SO_REUSEADDR lets a new listening socket bind anyway; it doesn't let two live servers share the port (that's SO_REUSEPORT )."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Reading Packets with Wireshark","f":"p6-wireshark-basics.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"You capture on a busy server and need the file to stay small. Which kind of filter achieves that?","o":["A capture (BPF) filter given to tcpdump","A Wireshark display filter","Either; they're the same thing","Neither; use -nn"],"a":0,"w":"Only a capture filter stops packets being recorded. A display filter hides packets in a file that already contains them."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Reading Packets with Wireshark","f":"p6-wireshark-basics.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which display filter shows only new connection attempts (the first packet of each handshake)?","o":["tcp.flags.syn == 1","tcp port 80","tcp.flags.syn == 1 && tcp.flags.ack == 0","tcp.flags.ack == 0"],"a":2,"w":"The SYN-ACK also has SYN set, so syn == 1 alone shows both halves. Requiring ACK clear leaves only the client's opening SYN. tcp port 80 is BPF syntax."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Reading Packets with Wireshark","f":"p6-wireshark-basics.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"In tcpdump output, what does Flags [P.] mean?","o":["A packet that was dropped","PSH and ACK: a segment carrying data, which also acknowledges","A ping","The last packet of the connection"],"a":1,"w":"P is PSH (deliver this data to the application now) and . is ACK. Most data segments look like this."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Reading Packets with Wireshark","f":"p6-wireshark-basics.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why add -nn to tcpdump?","o":["It captures full packets instead of headers","It enables IPv6","It prints packets in hex","It stops tcpdump resolving addresses to hostnames and ports to service names, so you see the real numbers and it doesn't make DNS lookups"],"a":3,"w":"Name resolution slows output, generates its own DNS traffic into your capture, and replaces 443 with \"https\". -X prints hex; -s 0 (the modern default) captures full packets."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Reading Packets with Wireshark","f":"p6-wireshark-basics.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does Follow → TCP Stream show?","o":["The application bytes of one connection, reassembled in order, both directions","Every packet in the capture, in order","The route the packets took","Only the TCP headers"],"a":0,"w":"Wireshark puts the payloads of one 5-tuple's segments back in sequence order and shows client and server bytes in two colours. It also sets the display filter to tcp.stream eq N ."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"Layering & Encapsulation","f":"p7-layering.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"In what order do headers appear in an HTTP request frame, from the first byte on the wire?","o":["HTTP, TCP, IP, Ethernet","Ethernet, IP, TCP, then the HTTP data","IP, Ethernet, TCP, HTTP","TCP, IP, Ethernet, HTTP"],"a":1,"w":"The outermost wrapper is added last by the sender and comes first on the wire: the link header, then IP, then TCP, then the application bytes."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"Layering & Encapsulation","f":"p7-layering.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A packet crosses an ordinary router (no NAT). What changes?","o":["Nothing at all","The TCP ports","The source and destination IP addresses","The Ethernet header is replaced, and the IP TTL is decremented (and the IPv4 checksum recomputed)"],"a":3,"w":"MAC addresses are per hop, so the frame is rebuilt. IP addresses and the transport header pass through unchanged, except for TTL and checksum."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"Layering & Encapsulation","f":"p7-layering.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"With a 1500-byte MTU, IPv4 and a TCP header without options, what's the maximum TCP payload per segment?","o":["1500 bytes","1480 bytes","1460 bytes","1518 bytes"],"a":2,"w":"MSS = MTU − IP header (20) − TCP header (20) = 1460. With Linux's default 12-byte timestamp option it's 1448."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"Layering & Encapsulation","f":"p7-layering.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"How does a receiving host know the payload of an IP packet is TCP and not UDP?","o":["The IP header's Protocol field: 6 for TCP, 17 for UDP","It inspects the payload for a TCP header","From the destination port","From the Ethernet header's EtherType"],"a":0,"w":"Every header names its payload's type for the layer above. EtherType says \"IPv4\", IP's Protocol says \"TCP\", and the port says which socket."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"Layering & Encapsulation","f":"p7-layering.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"After a VPN goes live, SSH and small requests work but large downloads hang. What's the most likely cause?","o":["The VPN encrypts large packets more slowly","A path MTU black hole: oversized packets are dropped and the ICMP \"fragmentation needed\" replies are blocked","TCP can't run inside a tunnel","DNS is failing for large responses"],"a":1,"w":"The tunnel lowers the MTU. Full-size packets with \"don't fragment\" set are dropped, and if ICMP is filtered the sender never learns to send smaller ones. Small packets fit, so interactive traffic still works."}];
